      "evidence": "The Definition says the exact value λ is read through the singleton {λ} with probability μ^A_ρ({λ}), then says an exact value of a quantity with no eigenvalues is “an idealization outside the assignment.” Its own outcome law is defined on Borel sets of ℝ, and pthm-nrqm-observables-position-and-momentum-outcome-laws, clause (d), says atomlessness “does not remove ℝ from the law's Borel outcome space.” Zero probability for every singleton means no point masses; it does not remove real values from the outcome space or law. Lack of a normalizable eigenvector does not establish that stronger claim.",
